  "summary": "Manjushas are an Indian art form.  They are temple-shaped boxes comprising eight pillars.",
  "description": "Manjushas are an Indian art form.  They are temple-shaped boxes comprising eight pillars. They are made of bamboo, jute, and paper. They also contain paintings of Hindu gods and goddesses and other characters. These boxes are used in Bishahari puja, a festival dedicated to the Snake Goddess Bishahari that is celebrated in Bhagalpur and the Anga Pradesh region also known as Angika Belt and nearby regions, India.",
  "culturalContext": "Registered as a Geographical Indication of India (handicraft, Bihar; registration 2021–22), which protects the name for products made in its place of origin by traditional methods.",
